Coral reef fish surveys, Florida Reef Tract
===========================================

Citation:

NOAA Southeast Fisheries Science Center; NOAA National Centers for Coastal Ocean
Science (2018). National Coral Reef Monitoring Program: Assessment of coral reef
fish communities in the Florida Reef Tract. NOAA National Centers for
Environmental Information. Dataset. https://doi.org/10.7289/v52n50ks

Rights:

Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal (CC0-1.0). Public domain dedication, so
these files can be redistributed freely. Cite it anyway.

Source:

https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/erddap/info/CRCP_Reef_Fish_Surveys_Florida/index.html

Note:

The files here are a curated subset. The full dataset on the ERDDAP server is
roughly 770 MB and 3.8 million rows, because it records every species at every
survey in every length bin, including absences. About 86% of those rows are
zeros. get_reef_fish_data.R documents exactly how these files were produced from
it.

Surveys ran in 2014, 2016, 2018, 2021, 2022 and 2024, across three regions of the
Florida Reef Tract: the Dry Tortugas, the Keys from Key West to Miami, and Miami
north to Martin County. Divers count and size fish inside a 7.5 m radius cylinder.


Files
-----

NCRMP_reef_fish_surveys.csv       8,560 rows, one per survey
NCRMP_reef_fish_species.csv     171,200 rows, one per survey per species
NCRMP_reef_fish_species_list.csv     20 rows, species codes and names


NCRMP_reef_fish_surveys.csv
---------------------------

One row per survey. A survey is a station within a primary sample unit in a given
year, so survey_id is what makes a row unique. Note that a sample unit usually
contains two stations, which are separate surveys a few metres apart.

  survey_id              PRIMARY_SAMPLE_UNIT, STATION_NR and YEAR joined by "_"
  PRIMARY_SAMPLE_UNIT    sampling location code
  STATION_NR             station within the sample unit
  YEAR                   2014, 2016, 2018, 2021, 2022 or 2024
  latitude, longitude    decimal degrees
  DEPTH                  metres, 0.76 to 31.9
  UNDERWATER_VISIBILITY  metres, 3 to 50.5
  HABITAT_TYPE           reef structure and vertical relief, 12 categories
  ZONE_NAME              Inshore, Forereef, and so on
  SUB_REGION_NAME        Biscayne, Upper Keys, Tortugas--Tortugas Bank, etc.
  REGION_DESCRIPTION     one of the three regions above
  PROT                   protection code, 0, 1 or 2. See the warning below
  MPA_NAME               marine protected area, or "Unprotected"
  total_abundance        all fish counted, summed over species and length bins
  species_richness       number of species recorded, 0 to 68

There are no missing values in this file.


NCRMP_reef_fish_species.csv
---------------------------

The 20 most frequently recorded species, one row per survey per species, with
zeros kept so that absences are visible.

  survey_id    joins to NCRMP_reef_fish_surveys.csv
  SPECIES_CD   species code, joins to NCRMP_reef_fish_species_list.csv
  abundance    fish counted, summed over length bins

Deliberately narrow. Join it to the surveys file to get depth, habitat and the
rest:

  surveys <- read.csv("data/Reef_fish/NCRMP_reef_fish_surveys.csv")
  species <- read.csv("data/Reef_fish/NCRMP_reef_fish_species.csv")

  both <- merge(species, surveys, by = "survey_id")


Two things to know before you use it
------------------------------------

1. PROT is a code, not a quantity.

   It takes values 0, 1 and 2, and R will store it as an integer and happily
   average it for you. The mean is meaningless. It also does not line up neatly
   with MPA_NAME: some rows with PROT = 2 are labelled "Unprotected". If
   protection status matters to your question, work from MPA_NAME, or check the
   NCRMP protocol documentation before trusting PROT.

2. abundance is not a whole number.

   Values like 0.12 and 0.5 appear because these are density estimates rather
   than raw head counts. Do not assume you can treat them as integer counts
   without thinking about it.


Some questions you could ask
----------------------------

  Does species richness differ among the three regions, or among habitat types?
  Has richness changed between 2014 and 2024?
  Is there more fish inside marine protected areas than outside?
  How does depth relate to what lives there?
  Which of the 20 common species are found together?

One result worth chasing: underwater visibility is a strong positive predictor of
species richness. Before concluding anything ecological, ask whether that is
about the reef or about how far the diver could see. Distinguishing the thing you
are measuring from the way you measured it is the whole of chapter 1.
